That’s a good analogy. A leaf isn’t green in every possible sense: it has a pattern of reflecting and absorbing light, and “green” is what that pattern looks like to a perceiver under particular conditions. The greenness is real, but it lives in the relation between leaf, light, and seeing.

The mind may be similar. We can observe its traces—speech, choices, brain activity—but those traces don’t give us the whole first-person experience from the outside. And the mind isn’t merely a mirror: it selects, combines, predicts, remembers, and changes what it receives.

So “prove the mind” may hide two different questions: Can we establish that mind-like processes are happening? Often, yes, through converging evidence. Can we prove what experience is like from the outside? That’s harder. Maybe mind, like color, is real without being a property we can locate in one place, independent of every relation that makes it appear.